.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="plot_haldane_phase_transition exposes the Haldane model&#x27;s chiral edge states on a ribbon -- periodic in-plane, open along one crystallographic direction. That is the easiest cut to compute, but it leaves open whether the edge state depends on cutting along a lattice direction the way graphene&#x27;s zigzag zero modes do (plot_graphene_zigzag_flake). It does not: a nonzero Chern number guarantees a chiral state on any boundary of the sample, however it is shaped. This example carves a circular disk out of the infinite lattice -- a boundary with no crystallographic meaning at all -- using build_finite_cluster&#x27;s keep predicate, and shows the in-gap states still hug that boundary in the topological phase, and fail to in the trivial one.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/condensed/topology/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_haldane_edge_state_map_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/condensed/topology/plot_haldane_edge_state_map`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">The Haldane Model: a Chiral Edge State on an Arbitrary Boundary</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/condensed/topology/plot_haldane_edge_state_map`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="plot_z2_topological_insulator computes the bulk \mathbb{Z}_2 invariant of the Kane-Mele model directly from its Bloch Hamiltonian. This example instead looks at what that invariant predicts on a finite sample. Whenever the Rashba term vanishes, s_z is conserved and kane_mele_hamiltonian is exactly block-diagonal in spin -- two independent Haldane models with opposite next-nearest-neighbor phase, \phi and -\phi. Building each spin&#x27;s finite flake separately with build_finite_cluster (the same disk-shaped cut used in plot_haldane_edge_state_map) shows both spins independently host a boundary-hugging state at the same real-space locations -- a Kramers pair -- while a momentum-resolved ribbon dispersion shows the two spins cross the gap with opposite group velocity, the helical counter-propagation that makes the quantum spin Hall edge robust against time-reversal-preserving disorder.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/condensed/topology/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_kane_mele_edge_flake_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/condensed/topology/plot_kane_mele_edge_flake`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Kane-Mele: Helical Edge States from Two Time-Reversed Haldane Copies</div>
    </div>


